3) Traditional frontend frameworks
    Traditional frontend frameworks are JavaScript-based tools used to build the visual, interactive side of web applications — what the user sees and clicks on in their browser.

    The major ones:
    Component-Based Frameworks (most common today)

    React — by Meta, the most widely used. Builds UIs from reusable components.
    Vue.js — lighter and beginner-friendly, popular in Asia and Europe
    Angular — by Google, full-featured and opinionated, common in enterprise

    Meta-Frameworks (built on top of the above)

    Next.js — React + server-side rendering, the modern standard for production React apps
    Nuxt.js — same idea but for Vue
    SvelteKit — built on Svelte, a newer leaner alternative

    Older / Legacy (still used but less dominant)

    jQuery — the original, still runs on millions of older sites
    Backbone.js, Ember.js — largely replaced by the above


